1. Activate the virtual environment

You creat it with
py -m venv project-name

but creating it doesn't activate it. In Command Prompt, run:

project-name\Scripts\activate.bat

(If you're using PowerShell instead of cmd, use project-name\Scripts\Activate.ps1 instead.)

2. Confirm it's active

Once activated, your prompt should change to show the environment name in parentheses, like:

(project-name) C:\your\path>

That's the signal it's active — if you don't see that prefix, it isn't active.

3. Then install Django

py -m pip install Django

or once the venv is active, you can usually just use pip instead of py -m pip:

pip install Django

A couple of notes:

Run the activate command from the same folder where project-name lives (i.e., don't cd into project-name first — activate it from one level up, pointing into project-name\Scripts\).
If PowerShell gives you an error about scripts being disabled when you try to activate, that's a script-execution policy issue — let me know if you hit that and I can walk you through it.


Run server:
py manage.py runserver